    "turn_text": "of people watching on this modern video platform and et cetera. You raise a really interesting point about competitiveness and antitrust because in the old school world of regulation, boy, am I going into the weeds here, when you look at Does this company have too much market power? There are two regulatory questions that the Federal Trade Commission or the Department of Justice looks at. What's the relevant geographic market and what's the relevant product market? Well, nowadays you brought up some examples. Those questions are super difficult. It's not even relevant anymore to ask what the relevant Geographic market and product. Who knows? It's constantly morphing. If you're in competition, you know, in your TikTok, what are you up against? Are you up against all television? Are you up against short-form video ads? Are you up against flash mobs? I don't even know. When you talk to your former colleagues in the Senate, and as you say, many of them still struggle with the, Hey, could my aid print out my email for me? Do they, do they have any thoughts about how to grapple with all of this, especially as like crypto is melting down? Like, is there a consensus emerging about how you regulate this new frontier?",
