  {
    "episode_id": 43480,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/3/43480.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 23,
    "turn_text": "No, I don't think there is. I think there's a lot of questions. And, and to me, that the most important thing you can do is ask a lot of questions. Don't assume that you've got all the answers to begin with. Think very strategically. And I think there's a problem with folks too often they get pride of authorship. I have a daughter full disclosure works in the tech industry and she said that's interesting because I have to explain political world to my colleagues because they say, Well, here they said this, but we gave them all this information and they didn't change their mind. And her point was, because very hard, when somebody says something in the political world to get them to change their mind, because in the tech world, when you say, okay, we need to do this. And then someone says, wait a minute, that doesn't make sense. It's not going to work with this. People go, oh, okay, let's figure out a workaround or let's, you know, here's the goal. And so I think that the question is, it always tell people, there's two questions I always ask. What is it that you're trying to accomplish? What in a perfect world would look the way you think it should look? What are you trying to accomplish? And then what's the theory of your case that that's a good thing? And so I think that that there's a lot of my colleagues that I talked to and God knows I love many of the people who have been advancing this dialogue because I think it's an important dialogue to advance, but by the same token, I also think that. They need to be less defensive and more responsive to people who say, what about this? What about? I'll give you this scenario. What about it? And when the answer is, just trust us, that's not an answer that's going to work very well for a lot of members.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "One should not assume they already have all the answers.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The two key questions to ask are: 'What are you trying to accomplish?' and 'What is your theory of change?'",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Asking many questions is the most important thing one can do.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Responding with 'just trust us' is ineffective.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "It is difficult to change minds in the political world.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "There is a lot of unanswered questions.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "In the tech world, people respond better to logical challenges.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Strategic thinking is essential.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
